WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:10.509
Earlier, we have talked about a few amazing
algorithms to teach digital creatures to walk.

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:15.309
And this time, we're interested in controlling
the joints of a digital character to not only

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:21.050
walk properly, but take into consideration
its surroundings. This new version can navigate

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:28.430
in 3D with static and dynamic, moving obstacles,
or even dribble a ball toward a target. Loving

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.259
the execution and the production value of
this paper.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:36.470
This is accomplished by an efficient system
that consists of two controllers that are

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.670
represented by learning algorithms.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:44.109
One, the low level controller is about about
maintaining balance and proper limb control

00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:49.579
by manipulating the joint positions and velocities
appropriately. This controller operates on

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:55.639
a fine time scale, thirty times per second
and is trained via a 4-layer neural network.

00:00:55.639 --> 00:01:01.120
Two, the high level controller can accomplish
bigger overarching goals, such as following

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.110
a path, or avoiding static and dynamic obstacles.
We don't need to run this so often, therefore

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:13.660
to save resources, this controller operates
on a coarse time scale, only twice each second

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.220
and is trained via a deep convolutional neural
network.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.990
It also has support for a a small degree of
transfer learning. Transfer learning means

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:26.630
that after successfully learning to solve
a problem, we don't have to start from scratch

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:32.130
for the next one, but we can reuse some of
that valuable knowledge and get a headstart.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.970
This is a heavily researched area and is likely
going to be one of the major next frontiers

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.900
in machine learning research. Now, make no
mistake, it is not like transfer learning

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.680
is suddenly to be considered a solved problem
- but in this particular case, it is finally

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.660
a possibility. Really cool!

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.250
I hope this brief exposé fired you up too.
This paper is a bomb, make sure to have a

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.590
look, as always, the link is available in
the video description.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.159
And by the way, with your support on Patreon,
we will soon be able to spend part of our

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.730
budget on empowering research projects. How
amazing is that?

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:11.510
The new Two Minute Papers shirts are also
flying off the shelves! Happy to hear you're

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.140
enjoying them so much. If you're interested,
hit up http://twominutepapers.com if you're

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:22.080
located in the US. The EU and worldwide store's
link is also available in the video description.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.760
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

